    "message": {
      "content": "# Distributed Tensor and Expert Parallel Inference: A Technical Note\n\n## 1. Overview and Motivation\n\nModern large models exceed the memory and compute capacity of a single accelerator. Two complementary parallelism strategies address this during inference:\n\n- **Tensor Parallelism (TP)**: Splits individual weight matrices (and activations) across devices. Every device holds a *shard* of every layer and processes the *same* tokens.\n- **Expert Parallelism (EP)**: Splits the *experts* of a Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) layer across devices. Each device holds a few complete experts; different tokens are routed to different devices.\n\nIn practice, serving stacks (vLLM, TensorRT-LLM, SGLang, DeepSpeed-Inference, Megatron-style runtimes) combine them: dense layers use TP, MoE layers use EP (often with TP *within* an expert, called ETP or expert-tensor sharding). The dominant cost in both cases is **communication**, so the design goal is to overlap and minimize collectives while preserving exact numerical semantics.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Tensor Parallel Inference\n\n### 2.1 Sharding scheme\n\nConsider a transformer layer with:\n\n- Attention projection: $Y = XW_Q, XW_K, XW_V$ then $A = \\text{softmax}(QK^T/\\sqrt{d})V$, output proj $A W_O$\n- MLP: $H = \\sigma(XW_1)$, $Y = H W_2$\n\nThe canonical Megatron-style split:\n\n| Weight | Shard dimension | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| $W_Q, W_K, W_V$ | Column (output dim), by head | Each device computes a subset of attention heads independently \u2014 no communication needed inside attention |\n| $W_O$ | Row (input dim) | Each device's attention output is a partial sum over its heads |\n",
      "reasoning": "",
      "role": "assistant"
    },
